      "widgets_values": [
        "http://localhost:8000/v1",
        "MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3",
        "subject_definitions:\n<Subject 1>: The replacement subject shown in <Picture 1>. Derive the subject's identity, facial features, hairstyle or other visible surface features, clothing, accessories, proportions, and visual appearance from the supplied image. Preserve these reference-defined traits consistently throughout the clip. Preserve the clothing material shown in <Picture 1>, including its texture, roughness, sheen, thickness, and drape. Keep these properties consistent throughout the clip, allowing reflections and folds to respond naturally to the lighting and motion in <Video 1>.\n<Picture 1>: Appearance reference for <Subject 1>. Its background, pose, framing, and lighting do not define the target scene.\n<Video 1>: Source of the main subject's actions, position, timing, camera movement, background, lighting, and shot sequence. Replace only that subject's appearance.\n\nsummary:\nRe-create the action timeline of <Video 1> once, replacing its main subject with <Subject 1> wherever the main subject is visible. Use <Picture 1> for appearance and <Video 1> for motion and scene structure.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1>: fully_preserved — maintain the same reference-defined identity and appearance through movement, turns, occlusions, and source cuts.\n<Picture 1>: attribute_transfer — transfer the depicted subject's appearance without transferring the image's background, static pose, framing, or lighting.\n<Video 1>: partially_preserved — preserve action order, timing, composition, background, lighting, camera movement, other subjects, and existing cuts while replacing the main subject's appearance.\n\ndetailed_description:\n[Shot 1] Beginning with the first visible frame, <Subject 1> occupies the main subject's position in <Video 1> and follows the source actions in chronological order. Adapt the motion to the reference subject's anatomy and proportions while preserving the source action and timing. Maintain the appearance defined by <Picture 1> after every turn, occlusion, and existing source cut. Preserve the source environment, other subjects, camera movement, and lighting, integrating the replacement with consistent perspective, contact, shadows, and occlusion. Follow the source timeline once through its final action and composition. Do not loop the opening, invent additional actions or cuts, or revert to the original subject's appearance.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nReference the timing and environmental sound characteristics of <Video 1>. Do not introduce new dialogue or unrelated sound effects. Exact original soundtrack preservation requires separate audio handling.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nReference any existing music in <Video 1>; do not introduce unrelated music.",
        "",
        1344,
        768,
        24,
        4
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "url": "http://localhost:8000/v1",
        "model": "MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3",
        "prompt": "subject_definitions:\n<Subject 1>: The replacement subject shown in <Picture 1>. Derive the subject's identity, facial features, hairstyle or other visible surface features, clothing, accessories, proportions, and visual appearance from the supplied image. Preserve these reference-defined traits consistently throughout the clip. Preserve the clothing material shown in <Picture 1>, including its texture, roughness, sheen, thickness, and drape. Keep these properties consistent throughout the clip, allowing reflections and folds to respond naturally to the lighting and motion in <Video 1>.\n<Picture 1>: Appearance reference for <Subject 1>. Its background, pose, framing, and lighting do not define the target scene.\n<Video 1>: Source of the main subject's actions, position, timing, camera movement, background, lighting, and shot sequence. Replace only that subject's appearance.\n\nsummary:\nRe-create the action timeline of <Video 1> once, replacing its main subject with <Subject 1> wherever the main subject is visible. Use <Picture 1> for appearance and <Video 1> for motion and scene structure.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1>: fully_preserved — maintain the same reference-defined identity and appearance through movement, turns, occlusions, and source cuts.\n<Picture 1>: attribute_transfer — transfer the depicted subject's appearance without transferring the image's background, static pose, framing, or lighting.\n<Video 1>: partially_preserved — preserve action order, timing, composition, background, lighting, camera movement, other subjects, and existing cuts while replacing the main subject's appearance.\n\ndetailed_description:\n[Shot 1] Beginning with the first visible frame, <Subject 1> occupies the main subject's position in <Video 1> and follows the source actions in chronological order. Adapt the motion to the reference subject's anatomy and proportions while preserving the source action and timing. Maintain the appearance defined by <Picture 1> after every turn, occlusion, and existing source cut. Preserve the source environment, other subjects, camera movement, and lighting, integrating the replacement with consistent perspective, contact, shadows, and occlusion. Follow the source timeline once through its final action and composition. Do not loop the opening, invent additional actions or cuts, or revert to the original subject's appearance.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nReference the timing and environmental sound characteristics of <Video 1>. Do not introduce new dialogue or unrelated sound effects. Exact original soundtrack preservation requires separate audio handling.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nReference any existing music in <Video 1>; do not introduce unrelated music.",
        "negative_prompt": "",
        "width": 1344,
